GAS SENSOR MONITOR (ESP32 + MQ5 + LCD)

Early-stage embedded project: reads gas concentration from an analog sensor
and displays air quality status on a 16x2 I2C LCD.

This is a first commit - a minimal "read input, show output" version, meant
as a base to build on (thresholds, calibration, alerts, etc. will come
later).

WHAT IT DOES
- Polls the gas sensor every 1 second (non-blocking, via millis())
- Prints the raw analog reading to Serial (115200 baud) for debugging
- Shows "Gaz detected" or "Normal air qlty" on the LCD based on a fixed
  threshold

HARDWARE
- MQ5 gas sensor  -> Analog pin 34
- LCD (I2C, 16x2) -> I2C bus, address 0x27

HOW THE THRESHOLD WORKS
analogRead() returns a raw value (0-4095 on ESP32's 12-bit ADC). The code
currently treats anything below 500 as "gas detected." This is a
placeholder - MQ-series sensors need a warm-up period (usually 24-48h for
first use, ~1-2 min per power-on afterward) and ideally a calibrated
baseline in clean air before this threshold means much.

SETUP
1. Wire the components as described above.
2. Install the LiquidCrystal_I2C library (via PlatformIO or Arduino Library
   Manager).
3. Flash the code to your ESP32.
4. Open Serial Monitor at 115200 baud to watch raw sensor values.
5. If the LCD shows nothing, confirm the I2C address (0x27 is common, but
   run an I2C scanner sketch if unsure) and check SDA/SCL wiring.

KNOWN LIMITATIONS / NEXT STEPS
- Fixed threshold (500) isn't calibrated - should be tuned per sensor and
  environment
- No sensor warm-up handling
- No smoothing/averaging of readings (raw ADC values can be noisy)
- sendUptime() is a placeholder name - doesn't actually send uptime, just
  reads and displays. Consider renaming (e.g. updateReading()) for clarity
- No unit tests / no persistent logging yet



## Testing

Added a `testing` branch to simulate sensor behavior without physical hardware:
- Pushes fake/false air quality data ("gaz detected" / "normal air quality") directly to Firebase Realtime Database to test the read/write logic and the Android app's live data display, without needing the ESP32 connected.

## Next Steps

- Connect the ESP32 to WiFi
- Read real sensor data and push it online to the Realtime Database